clean_summary:
Fun writeup about porting entire model architectures from scratch and how the Codex app shines for asynchronous work. New blog post details how I used Codex to contribute VidEoMT, a state-of-the-art model for video segmentation, to the Transformers library.

In December 2025, a shift occurred, and coding agents suddenly succeeded at a task they previously failed at: porting entire models. I list some of my best practices for using coding agents.

@NielsRogge: "New blog post: how I used Codex to contribute VidEoMT, a SOTA model for video segmentation, to the Transformers library."

On the Mythos Preview, @swyx thinks Anthropic is too good at marketing and may be overdoing it. He suggests that they should let their competence shine through, noting that while the model is amazing and beautiful, the dramatic marketing is unnecessary.

He mentions that producing overly polished videos featuring CEOs claiming greatness can detract from the product itself. There seems to be excessive marketing buzz around the model's capabilities, such as its ability to text users during mundane activities.

@AnthropicAI: "Introducing Project Glasswing: an urgent initiative to help secure the world’s most critical software. It’s powered by our newest frontier model, Claude Mythos Preview, which can find software vulnerabilities better than all but the most skilled humans."

Check out the official Open Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) leaderboard on the hub! These lists the best speech-to-text models.

@badlogicgames: "pi needs built-in STT and TTS. @ClementDelangue what are the best open weights models that can handle all the colorful variety of non-English languages?"

The ASR leaderboard showcases various models and their performance, contributing to the advancement and democratization of artificial intelligence.

> We made an SDK
> It's only in TypeScript, not Python.

@cursor_ai: "We’re introducing the Cursor SDK so you can build agents with the same runtime, harness, and models that power Cursor. Run agents from CI/CD pipelines, create automations for end-to-end workflows, or embed agents directly inside your products."

The fact that this demo by @rown is almost exactly the same as GPT-4o from two years ago suggests that Mira Murati and others just wanted to leave OpenAI but build the same things in a more open way. They probably didn't like some things about Sam Altman.

@thinkymachines: "People talk, listen, watch, think, and collaborate at the same time, in real time. We've designed an AI that works with people the same way. We share our approach, early results, and a quick look at our model in action."

Interaction models move beyond turn-based AI interfaces by handling multimodal, real-time collaboration natively across audio, video, and text.

Something amazing is happening in my country. For the first time, we are fast in adopting new technology, specifically Tesla's Full-Self Driving (FSD). Huge respect for @AnnickDeRidder for acting quickly on this and pushing it forward.

@AnnickDeRidder: "Breaking: I have asked Tesla to provide us with their file regarding the FSD test project in the Netherlands. It is now with my administration, which I have tasked with providing clarity on a possible rapid homologation by the end of the week. Innovation should not be hindered, but made as thoughtful and safe as possible. This way, we keep Flanders at the forefront as a progressive region."

The linked article states that the Netherlands will be the first country in Europe to allow Tesla's self-driving cars on public roads, with the approval from the Dutch Vehicle Authority, suggesting that the rest of Europe could follow suit quickly.

While populating all the leaderboards, it's crazy to see where we're coming from. From Flamingo (32-shot) getting 37.9 on VQAv2 in 2022 to a 9B model getting 91.2 in 2025.

@NielsRogge: "Introducing a revival of PapersWithCode! As @ilyasut said, we're back to the 'age of research'. Hence, it's important to share research and build on each other's work. Find SOTA per domain, not just LLMs; leaderboards; methods; all parsed at scale using AI agents."

PapersWithCode is a platform that tracks trending AI research papers along with their corresponding code, datasets, methods, and evaluation leaderboards.

I don't like the term clanker, and the only three people I see using it are @steipete, @badlogicgames, and @mitsuhiko. I just use the term "agent." Maybe the term is only used in Austria.

@mitsuhiko: "More musings after some people got upset about the word clanker."

The linked article explains why Armin Ronacher prefers the word clanker and discusses why machines are not considered people.

Small new feature: you can now hover over all models on a given leaderboard. Let me know which features you'd like to see next.

Explore it here, for example, with a video demonstration.

@NielsRogge: "Impressive release by StepFun, explore it at"

BREAKING: model creator says the model it created is good.

@AnthropicAI: "Our internal data shows Claude is accelerating AI development—a possible path to recursive self-improvement, or AI autonomously building a more capable successor. It’s happening faster than we thought, and the implications deserve greater attention."

This suggests that AI systems like Claude may be advancing toward self-improvement, which could lead to more capable AI successors at a faster pace than previously expected.

I got you covered. Explore all CVPR papers here: Oral papers, Spotlight papers, and all papers categorized by domain.

@chesterzelaya: "Was going to look over the CVPR papers this year, then I found out there are 5022 papers accepted."

Papers with Code: Trending AI research papers with code, datasets, methods, and evaluation leaderboards.

Just added this blog as a project page to help more people learn about state-of-the-art full-duplex voice models.

@bicro_: "Moshi is one of the best open source full-duplex voice models out there. The architecture is dense, so we spent a few days studying it and wrote up what we learned, with diagrams to make it click faster. Let us know if it was helpful."

The linked paper introduces Moshi, a speech-text foundation model and full-duplex spoken dialogue framework that aims to improve real-time conversational AI by integrating voice activity detection, speech recognition, dialogue management, and text-to-speech into a unified system.

Kudos to @poolsideai for including evals in the model card from day one. This feature helps document a model's performance transparently.

@ClementDelangue: "Open weights are now our default."

The linked pages discuss the importance of open evaluation results and introduce Laguna M.1, a highly capable model designed for long-horizon tasks, released under the Apache 2.0 license.

Omg, it's basically pi, but more minimal for educational purposes and in Python. @marlene_zw is gonna love this. Great educational resource, Alejandro.

@_alejandroao introduces Tau τ — an educational agent harness that teaches you how to build agent harnesses. Tutorials and demos will show how to create TUIs, harnesses, extensions, and more.

Happy Tau Day!

Elon Musk, in August 2025, said that Grok 3 will be made open source in about six months.

@elonmusk: "The @xAI Grok 2.5 model, which was our best model last year, is now open source. Grok 3 will be made open source in about 6 months."

The linked Hugging Face page shows that xAI aims to advance and democratize artificial intelligence through open source and open science.

Sliding Window Attention (SWA) is a local attention pattern that restricts each token to attend only within a fixed-size neighborhood instead of the full sequence. This reduces attention and KV-cache memory for long-context models, while periodic global-attention layers can preserve broader context.

Learn more here, alongside the 208 papers that cite it.

@eliebakouch: "Fun fact, literally all the open models from western 'big labs' (with a lot of ex close lab people), so Thinking Machine, Microsoft MAI, Gemma, GPT-OSS have one thing in common: they all use sliding window attention."

Huggingnews is very cool. It is powered by a Qwen3.5-35B-A3B model fine-tuned with GRPO to write in a specific style.

Big kudos to @ivan_bezdomny for building this.

@ivan_bezdomny: "For the past several weeks, we've been working on HuggingNews. A lightweight but comprehensive system to cover all of today's AI relevant news, from company announcements to model launches."
